; Instruction-following curriculum
; Bridges from example-based synthesis to instruction-conditioned execution.
; Requires: nl_helpers.selph (first_word, second_word, last_word)
;
; Core mechanism: the `dispatch` builtin looks up a macro by name (a string)
; in the current environment and applies it. The library IS the dispatch
; table — each promoted macro is an entry. The system learns:
;   1. Individual operations (promotes named macros)
;   2. Instruction following via (dispatch (first_word x) (last_word x))
;
; This is analogous to `map`: the system doesn't learn HOW dispatch works,
; it learns WHAT to dispatch on and HOW to parse instructions.

; === Stage 0: Teach individual operations ===
; Each task produces a named macro that dispatch can look up.
; Task names = operation keywords used in instructions.
; Solutions are direct builtin wrappers: (string-reverse x), etc.

(task "reverse" 2
  ("hello" "olleh")
  ("world" "dlrow")
  ("abc" "cba")
  ("moon" "noom")
  ("fog" "gof")
  ("sky" "yks"))

(task "upper" 2
  ("hello" "HELLO")
  ("world" "WORLD")
  ("abc" "ABC")
  ("moon" "MOON")
  ("fog" "FOG")
  ("sky" "SKY"))

(task "lower" 2
  ("HELLO" "hello")
  ("WORLD" "world")
  ("ABC" "abc")
  ("MOON" "moon")
  ("FOG" "fog")
  ("SKY" "sky"))

(task "len" 2
  ("hello" 5)
  ("world" 5)
  ("abc" 3)
  ("moon" 4)
  ("fog" 3)
  ("sky" 3)
  ("hi" 2)
  ("it" 2))

; === Stage 1: Two-word instruction dispatch ===
; Format: "OP ARG". The synthesizer should discover:
;   (dispatch (first_word x) (last_word x))
; This is a depth-2 composition: dispatch takes two depth-1 args.

(task "follow_str" 2
  ("reverse hello" "olleh")
  ("reverse moon" "noom")
  ("reverse abc" "cba")
  ("upper hello" "HELLO")
  ("upper moon" "MOON")
  ("upper abc" "ABC")
  ("lower HELLO" "hello")
  ("lower MOON" "moon"))

; Numeric dispatch: same mechanism, different output type.
(task "follow_num" 2
  ("len hello" 5)
  ("len moon" 4)
  ("len abc" 3)
  ("len fog" 3)
  ("len hi" 2)
  ("len it" 2))

; === Stage 2: Three-word NL instructions ===
; Format: "FILLER OP ARG". Now the operation is second_word, not first_word.
; Expected: (dispatch (second_word x) (last_word x))

(task "follow_nl" 2
  ("please reverse hello" "olleh")
  ("kindly reverse moon" "noom")
  ("now reverse abc" "cba")
  ("please upper hello" "HELLO")
  ("kindly upper moon" "MOON")
  ("now upper abc" "ABC")
  ("please lower HELLO" "hello")
  ("kindly lower MOON" "moon"))

; === Stage 3: Compositional instructions (HO decomposition) ===
; Element-wise operations on multi-word data. The split-map-join template
; in decompose.rs discovers these automatically:
;   (string-join (map string-reverse (string-split x " ")) " ")
; These show that once operations exist in the library, the system
; composes them over structured data without new primitives.

(task "reverse_words" 2
  ("hello moon" "olleh noom")
  ("abc fog" "cba gof")
  ("sky gem" "yks meg")
  ("hi no" "ih on")
  ("ice bay" "eci yab")
  ("den fog" "ned gof"))

(task "upper_words" 2
  ("hello moon" "HELLO MOON")
  ("abc fog" "ABC FOG")
  ("sky gem" "SKY GEM")
  ("hi no" "HI NO")
  ("ice bay" "ICE BAY")
  ("den fog" "DEN FOG"))

; === Design notes ===
;
; Why dispatch as a special form (not a regular builtin):
;   dispatch needs the dynamic environment to find promoted macros.
;   Regular builtins are fn(&[Value]) -> Result without env access.
;   As a special form in eval_inner, dispatch has the full env.
;
; Why task names = operation keywords:
;   The promoted macro name IS the dispatch key. When the curriculum
;   solves "reverse" and promotes (defmacro reverse (s) (string-reverse s)),
;   then (dispatch "reverse" "hello") finds it by name. The library
;   IS the dispatch table.
;
; Naming conflicts:
;   "reverse" and "length" shadow builtins within this curriculum run.
;   Using "len" instead of "length" to minimize shadowing. Domain
;   isolation (separate grow runs) prevents cross-contamination.
;
; Generalization path:
;   - Richer NL: "find the reverse of hello" needs keyword extraction
;     from arbitrary positions (string-contains as synth component).
;   - Parameterized ops: "take 3 from hello" needs numeric argument
;     parsing (parse-int builtin).
;   - Chained ops: "reverse then upper hello" needs sequential parsing.
;   - The endgame: dispatch over the full learned library. Every
;     synthesized operation becomes dispatchable by name. The library
;     grows, and the instruction vocabulary grows with it.
